<p>Residents in Morón, a town in Ciego de Ávila province in central Cuba, damaged a local Communist party office during a protest over prolonged power cuts and shortages of food, fuel and medicine. Authorities later arrested five people. The unrest underscored widening frustration beyond Havana, as Cuba’s energy crisis brings long daily blackouts and adds to broader economic strain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/QqdLsOO8stDuysOafVho-kPlCSFGW9ZTyErtlmUiO2kWvWGoo0arZ0a3hNUeNJdxydNDRWSQN7VoRSh4ehcCy7A-y3T4sq7oYeUtEL-SF_Ag-YImbGYM_XKVkyJPoTIYpd9K3WD4w7bCZj7AN5O9pYkjf5hUF-M8UOtumjwfZt5UoMbUQ_GcA5FGERuV7-ncW2aBt6JCLsx0yy7_FJNbOpcazvrBf_HIbzfwMfJ-ByZwpFiO3J6d5MTlGiUst3jCzNdyIuxzncZZ7G9x8rPJ' alt='Image accompanying coverage of arrests after the protest in Morón, Cuba' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Rare target: Attacks on Communist party property are unusual in Cuba, where public protests are rare and tightly controlled.</li><li>Small group: Multiple reports said a small group of residents, rather than a mass crowd, carried out the action.</li><li>Provincial setting: Morón is a little more than 300 miles, or about 500 kilometers, east of Havana, showing that the unrest was not limited to the capital.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuban state media: State-run newspaper Invasor said the gathering began peacefully but later &quot;degenerated into vandalism,&quot; casting the damage as criminal acts against public property. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/14/arrests-cuba-protest-local-communist-party-office'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Cuban authorities: Officials acknowledged talks with the United States aimed at defusing the situation, according to Deutsche Welle. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/cuba-protesters-attack-ruling-party-s-office-amid-blackouts/a-76363513'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c875zd7de5xo'>Cuban protesters ransack Communist office as energy crisis 